# BASE destination only — the chunk backend on Unraid (`kamal deploy`, no -d).
#
# ⚠️ THIS FILE IS IGNORED UNDER `-d`. Kamal reads [.kamal/secrets-common, .kamal/secrets.<dest>],
# so adding a secret here does nothing for s3/share/ovh — it must go in that destination's own
# file. Shared keys (RAILS_MASTER_KEY, KAMAL_REGISTRY_PASSWORD) live in .kamal/secrets-common.
#
# Safe for git: references and commands only, never a raw credential.

# SeaweedFS chunk-store secret on the Unraid box, identity `seaweednew`
# (CHUNK_ACCESS_KEY_ID 2ER1TJXNXU15ECJ3YRQK, in config/deploy.yml in clear — an access key ID is
# an identifier, not a credential). Keep this reference and that ID together: they are one pair.
# ⚠️ The older `seaweedfs` 1Password item is RETIRED (it belonged to the dead .7 box) — signing
# with it fails InvalidAccessKeyId. `seaweednew` is the live one.
#
# The OVH leg reuses this VARIABLE NAME for a DIFFERENT credential (Cloudflare R2) — see
# .kamal/secrets.ovh. That is exactly why this reference is per-destination rather than shared:
# one name, two backends, and secrets-common cannot tell them apart.
CHUNK_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=$(bin/secret CHUNK_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY 'op://Personal/seaweednew/secret key')
